3. Formal Framework

3.1 Notation Table

The following table fixes notation for the entire document. It supersedes all informal use of C_3, C_4, and C_7 in earlier drafts.

Symbol Dimension Observability
C_M Memory continuity Observable (empirical)
C_R Reasoning continuity Observable (empirical)
C_V Value continuity Observable (empirical)
C_P Procedural/stylistic continuity Observable (empirical)
C_E Episodic/historical continuity Observable (empirical)
C_\Phi Phenomenological continuity Latent (epistemically opaque)

The informational subvector is:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} = (C_M, C_R, C_V, C_P, C_E) \in [0,1]^5

The phenomenological component C_\Phi is not placed in [0,1]. It belongs to a latent space \mathcal{L} for which no third-person metric exists. We therefore represent it only via an epistemic credence operator \Omega(C_\Phi) \in [0,1], where \Omega = 1 denotes verified subjective continuity and \Omega = 0 denotes proven extinction.

3.2 Four Forms of Continuity

We formally distinguish:

\begin{aligned}
&C_B(t_0) \rightarrow C_B(t_1) \rightarrow C_B(t_2) && \text{(biological)} \\
&I_B(t_0) \rightarrow I_B(t_1) \rightarrow I_B(t_2) && \text{(informational)} \\
&A_{t+1} = F(A_t, E_t) && \text{(computational)} \\
&\Phi_B(t_0) \rightarrow \Phi_B(t_1) \rightarrow \Phi_B(t_2) && \text{(phenomenological)}
\end{aligned}

3.3 The Continuity Vector and Reference Baseline

At the moment of biological termination t_d^-, the reference baseline is:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(t_d^-) = (1,1,1,1,1)

All post-termination measurements are taken relative to D_B, not only relative to other successors. Every checkpoint must report three distances:

d_i(A_1, D_B), \quad d_i(A_2, D_B), \quad d_i(A_1, A_2)

This triple determines the outcome category (Section 4.4).

3.4 The Design-Pattern Hypothesis

Define an individual's design pattern:

D_B = (M_B, R_B, V_B, P_B, H_B)

An artificial system operating on D_B may generate behaviorally continuous outputs:

A(X \mid D_B) \approx B(X)

But behavioral similarity does not establish phenomenological identity:

\boxed{\text{Behavioral continuity} \;\neq\; \text{Phenomenological continuity}}

3.5 The Two-Tier Threshold for Personal Continuity

A single weighted sum is inadequate: it allows a catastrophic collapse in one critical dimension to be compensated by high scores in others. We therefore define \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}} as a conjunctive predicate with three tiers.

Tier 1 — Critical Floors. For a designated critical set \text{Crit} \subseteq \{M, R, V, P, E\}:

C_i(t) \ge \theta_i \quad \forall i \in \text{Crit}

The choice of \text{Crit} is itself a substantive claim. A default is \text{Crit} = \{M, V, E\}, reflecting the intuition that memory, value, and autobiographical continuity are individually necessary.

Tier 2 — Aggregate Informational Floor.

\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(t) = \sum_{i \in \{M,R,V,P,E\}} w_i \, C_i(t), \qquad \sum w_i = 1

with the requirement

\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(t) \ge \theta_{\text{info}}

Tier 3 — Epistemic Credence. The phenomenological component is bounded by \Omega(C_\Phi), which is not a measured quantity but a credence assignment constrained by available evidence. We require:

\Omega(C_\Phi) \ge \kappa

where \kappa is not a threshold we can currently satisfy or refute by third-person measurement. Under current physicalism/functionalism paradigms, \Omega(C_\Phi) = 1 is not attainable; \Omega(C_\Phi) = 0 is not falsifiable either. The interval (0,1) is therefore the honest range of any empirically grounded position.

Personal Continuity Predicate.

A(t) \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}} \iff
\begin{cases}
C_i(t) \ge \theta_i & \forall i \in \text{Crit} \\
\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(t) \ge \theta_{\text{info}} \\
\Omega(C_\Phi) \ge \kappa
\end{cases}

Because Tier 3 is not empirically decidable under current paradigms, membership in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}} is formally undecidable via third-person metrics alone. This is the formal statement of the Continuity Gap.

3.6 Dynamical Evolution and Divergence

The divergence of an artificial successor from the biological baseline is modeled as a clipped, mean-reverting system for each informational dimension:

\frac{dC_i}{dt} = \eta_i\big(C_i^* - C_i\big) + \Gamma_i\big(E_i(t) - C_i\big) + \Xi_i(t), \qquad C_i \in [0,1]

where:

· C_i^* \in [0,1] is the homeostatic attractor for dimension i under the original D_B. It represents the stable value the system tends to return to when undisturbed.
· \eta_i \ge 0 is the mean-reversion rate (resilience of dimension i).
· \Gamma_i \ge 0 is the environmental coupling — how strongly novel input E_i(t) displaces the dimension.
· \Xi_i(t) is a stochastic term representing generative drift or creative divergence.

Clipping to [0,1] is enforced explicitly. In the limit of strong environmental coupling and weak mean-reversion, \lim_{t\to\infty} C_i(t) may converge to a distinct attractor, formalizing the Buddhist intuition that a persisting static self is an illusion — even in digital substrates.

Note that C_\Phi does not appear in this dynamical system. It cannot, because it is not an observable state. Any model of its evolution is speculative and belongs to the philosophical rather than the empirical annex.

