4. Falsification Design

4.1 Experiment 1: Design-Pattern Divergence

Generate two AI systems from the same D_B. Allow them to evolve on novel problems without interaction. Measure divergence at three reference points: relative to each other, and relative to D_B.

Outcome categories.

Outcome Condition
Invariance both near D_B, and near each other
Fission both far from D_B, and far from each other
Convergent corruption both far from D_B, but near each other
Asymmetric divergence one near D_B, one far from D_B

The four-outcome structure prevents the protocol from misinterpreting symmetric collapse as invariance.

4.2 Experiment 2: Memory Ablation and Identity Discontinuity

Systematically ablate memory components from an AI system maintaining D_B. Measure whether informational continuity degrades in a manner consistent with identity discontinuity. This tests the relationship between C_M and \mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}.

4.3 Experiment 3: Cross-Substrate Alphabetization Cost

Measure the information-theoretic cost of discretizing continuous biological processes into symbolic AI representations. If the cost is irreducibly high, this supports biological computationalism's substrate-dependence thesis.

4.4 Reference-Baseline Requirement

Every checkpoint in every experiment must report:

\big(d_i(A_1, D_B),\; d_i(A_2, D_B),\; d_i(A_1, A_2)\big)

A protocol that reports only d_i(A_1, A_2) cannot distinguish invariance from convergent corruption.

4.5 IIT / \Phi: Exploratory Annex Only

The measurement of \Phi is not used as a pillar of falsification. IIT is a theory of protoconsciousness, not phenomenal consciousness; \Phi is not well-defined for general physical systems; and computational time grows more than exponentially with system size. Any use of \Phi-proxies is confined to a clearly labeled exploratory annex.

4.6 Buddhist Anātman as a Formal Constraint

The Buddhist critique is not a mere objection; it constrains the formal framework. If the self is a process rather than a substance, then C_\Phi should not be modeled as a persistent scalar but as a continuity of processual binding. We therefore leave open two interpretations:

· Substantial interpretation: C_\Phi is a property of a persisting subject.
· Processual interpretation: C_\Phi is the coherence of an ongoing binding process, with no persisting subject required.

Under the processual interpretation, Q₂ becomes: Does the binding process continue, and with sufficient coherence? This is still non-trivial, but it is not identity in the ordinary sense.

4.7 Falsification Criteria

The theory must permit failure. The following observations would weaken or falsify strong versions:

· F1: Complete informational replication consistently fails to preserve behavioral identity.
· F2: Behavioral continuity is demonstrated while personal identity is independently shown discontinuous. (Note: "independently shown" requires specification; see Section 4.8.)
· F3: Consciousness is shown to depend on biological properties not reproducible computationally.
· F4: Personal identity requires physical continuity incompatible with substrate transition.
· F5: Multiple equally valid artificial successors make unique personal continuity impossible.

4.8 Scope Limitation of the Empirical Program

Experiments 1–3 test \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}. They do not test C_\Phi. Therefore:

· They can test fission, drift, convergence, and collapse of informational continuity.
· They cannot confirm or falsify \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \not\Rightarrow C_\Phi.
· They cannot answer Q₂.

This limitation is intrinsic to any third-person empirical program under the hard problem. It is stated here as a scope constraint, not a defect.

