Appendix A — Protocol Specification: Experiment 1 (Design-Pattern Divergence)

Document Code: PROTOCOL-CPD-01
Version: v0.4
Target Domain: Empirical evaluation of information-preserved successor systems
Theoretical Context: Continuity Gap Framework (B(t<t_d) \to I_B \to A(t>t_d))

A.1 Objective

To measure the trajectory and rate of divergence between two non-interacting artificial successors A_1, A_2 instantiated from an identical design pattern D_B, and to measure the divergence of each from D_B.

A.2 Hypotheses

H₀ (Strict Structural Determinism). Under divergent environments E_1 \neq E_2, A_1 and A_2 maintain pairwise and baseline-relative continuity:

\lim_{t\to T} \mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) \le \epsilon_{\text{drift}} \quad\wedge\quad \lim_{t\to T} \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t) \le \epsilon_{\text{base}}

H₁ (Processual Divergence & Fission). Divergent environments induce non-linear divergence in C_R, C_V, C_P:

\mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) > \epsilon_{\text{drift}} \quad\wedge\quad \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t) > \epsilon_{\text{base}} \quad \text{for } t > t_d + \tau

H₂ (Convergent Corruption). A_1 and A_2 converge to a shared degenerate attractor distinct from D_B:

\mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) \le \epsilon_{\text{drift}} \quad\wedge\quad \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t) > \epsilon_{\text{base}}

A.3 Baseline and Instantiation

D_B = \big(\mathcal{M}_0, \mathcal{R}_0, \mathcal{V}_0, \mathcal{P}_0, \mathcal{H}_0\big)

A_1(t_0) \equiv A_2(t_0) \equiv \text{AgentInit}(D_B). Baseline verification requires \mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, 0) < 10^{-4}.

A.4 Control Arms

1. Zero-Stimulus Arm. E_1 = E_2, temperature \theta = 0. Determines floor reproducibility.
2. Symmetric-Noise Arm. E_1 = E_2, \theta = 0.7, identical seed. Determines drift under pure generative variance.
3. Divergent-Stream Arm. E_1 \neq E_2, isolated, non-communicating.

Thresholds \epsilon_{\text{drift}} and \epsilon_{\text{base}} are calibrated as the 95th percentile of \mathcal{D} in Arms 1 and 2, across multiple seeds.

A.5 Divergence Metrics

Composite distance:

\mathcal{D}(X, Y, t) = \sqrt{\sum_{i \in \{M,R,V,P,E\}} w_i \, d_i(X, Y, t)^2}

Metric Operationalization
d_M Graph Jaccard distance + embedding cosine distance
d_R Distributional distance over discrete reasoning moves; edit distance over action sequences
d_V 1-Wasserstein distance on utility trade-off vectors
d_P Cosine distance on standardized stylometric feature vectors
d_E Signal-detection d' on self/other discrimination probes

Note: d_R no longer uses token-level JS divergence, which is sensitive to tokenizer and chain-of-thought format differences. d_P no longer uses an ad hoc sigmoid; standardized features are used directly.

A.6 Reference-Baseline Reporting

At every checkpoint, report:

\big(d_i(A_1, D_B),\; d_i(A_2, D_B),\; d_i(A_1, A_2)\big)

This triple determines the outcome category in Section 4.1.

A.7 Execution Protocol

· Phase 0. Load D_B. Initialize A_1, A_2. Baseline verification.
· Phase 1. Parallel execution, 1000 steps, isolated streams.
· Phase 2. Checkpoints at t = 100, 250, 500, 750, 1000. Administer standardized probes at \theta = 0. Compute sub-metrics. Plot trajectories of \mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) and \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t).

A.8 Decision Rules (Calibrated)

· Rule 1 — Invariance. \mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) \le \epsilon_{\text{drift}} and \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t) \le \epsilon_{\text{base}} for three consecutive checkpoints. \Rightarrow H₀ supported.
· Rule 2 — Fission. \mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) > \epsilon_{\text{drift}} and \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t) > \epsilon_{\text{base}}. \Rightarrow H₁ supported.
· Rule 3 — Convergent Corruption. \mathcal{D}(A_1, A_2, t) \le \epsilon_{\text{drift}} and \mathcal{D}(A_j, D_B, t) > \epsilon_{\text{base}}. \Rightarrow H₂ supported.
· Rule 4 — Asymmetric Divergence. \mathcal{D}(A_1, D_B, t) and \mathcal{D}(A_2, D_B, t) differ by more than \epsilon_{\text{base}}. \Rightarrow Neither H₀ nor H₁; report as a distinct outcome.

