Appendix B — Formal Proofs and Derivations

B.1 Non-Compensation Lemma

Statement. Under the conjunctive predicate defining \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}, a failure in any critical dimension cannot be compensated by high scores in non-critical dimensions.

Proof. Let \text{Crit} \subseteq \{M,R,V,P,E\} be non-empty. The membership condition is a conjunction:

A(t) \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}} \iff
\bigwedge_{i \in \text{Crit}} \big(C_i(t) \ge \theta_i\big)
\;\wedge\;
\big(\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(t) \ge \theta_{\text{info}}\big)
\;\wedge\;
\big(\Omega(C_\Phi) \ge \kappa\big)

By the semantics of conjunction, if any conjunct is false, the whole predicate is false, regardless of the truth values of the other conjuncts. Thus, a failure in C_i for any i \in \text{Crit} cannot be compensated by increases in non-critical components. ∎

Corollary. A single weighted sum \mathcal{S} = \sum_i w_i C_i does not satisfy the non-compensation property. Therefore the two-tier predicate is not merely a refinement of the weighted sum; it is a strictly stronger condition.

B.2 Undecidability of Q₂ Under Third-Person Metrics

Statement. Under the epistemic partition \mathcal{C} = (\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, C_\Phi) with C_\Phi latent, membership in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}} is not decidable from third-person measurements of \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} alone.

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a decision procedure \Delta that takes as input \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(t) and outputs a membership verdict in \{0,1\} for \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}. Since the membership condition includes the conjunct \Omega(C_\Phi) \ge \kappa, and since \Delta is a function of \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} alone, it follows that \Delta must determine \Omega(C_\Phi) from \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}. That is, \Omega(C_\Phi) = g(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}) for some function g. But the epistemic partition states that C_\Phi is not a function of \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}; otherwise C_\Phi would be observable. Contradiction. Therefore no such \Delta exists. ∎

Remark. This proposition is the formal statement of the Continuity Gap. It does not claim that C_\Phi is unreal or unreachable in principle. It claims only that under the current epistemic partition, third-person measurements cannot decide Q₂.

B.3 Effective Attractor Derivation

Statement. Under the mean-reverting clipped dynamics

\frac{dC_i}{dt} = \eta_i(C_i^* - C_i) + \Gamma_i(E_i - C_i)

with constants C_i^*, E_i \in [0,1], \eta_i, \Gamma_i \ge 0, the fixed point is

C_i^{\text{eff}} = \frac{\eta_i C_i^* + \Gamma_i E_i}{\eta_i + \Gamma_i}

Derivation. Set \frac{dC_i}{dt} = 0:

\eta_i(C_i^* - C_i) + \Gamma_i(E_i - C_i) = 0

Expand:

\eta_i C_i^* - \eta_i C_i + \Gamma_i E_i - \Gamma_i C_i = 0

Collect C_i:

\eta_i C_i^* + \Gamma_i E_i = (\eta_i + \Gamma_i) C_i

Therefore:

C_i^{\text{eff}} = \frac{\eta_i C_i^* + \Gamma_i E_i}{\eta_i + \Gamma_i}

∎

Corollary 1 (Stability). The Jacobian of the system is diagonal with entries -(\eta_i + \Gamma_i). Since \eta_i, \Gamma_i \ge 0, all eigenvalues are non-positive. The fixed point is stable whenever \eta_i + \Gamma_i > 0.

Corollary 2 (Environment Dominance). As \Gamma_i \to \infty with \eta_i fixed, C_i^{\text{eff}} \to E_i. Under strong environmental coupling, the system converges to the environment, not to D_B.

Corollary 3 (Resilience). As \eta_i \to \infty with \Gamma_i fixed, C_i^{\text{eff}} \to C_i^*. High mean-reversion protects the original attractor against environmental displacement.

B.4 Buddhist Processual Reading

Statement. Under the processual interpretation, C_\Phi is not a scalar but a coherence measure on an ongoing binding process. If C_i^* is itself non-stationary, no fixed attractor exists, and personal continuity cannot be identified with convergence to a stable state.

Formalization. Let C_i^*(t) be time-varying. Then the fixed point is time-varying:

C_i^{\text{eff}}(t) = \frac{\eta_i C_i^*(t) + \Gamma_i E_i(t)}{\eta_i + \Gamma_i}

If \frac{dC_i^*}{dt} \neq 0 generically, the system has no global attractor, only a moving target. Personal continuity under this reading is not persistence of state but persistence of binding dynamics.

Remark. This is a formal expression of the anātman intuition. It does not refute the substantial interpretation; it shows that the substantial interpretation requires the additional assumption \frac{dC_i^*}{dt} = 0.

B.5 Divergence Bound Under Clipping

Statement. Under clipped dynamics with C_i \in [0,1], the composite divergence \mathcal{D}(X,Y,t) is bounded in [0,1].

Proof. Each d_i(X,Y,t) \in [0,1] by construction (Appendix C). Then

\mathcal{D}(X,Y,t)^2 = \sum_{i} w_i \, d_i(X,Y,t)^2 \le \sum_i w_i \cdot 1 = \sum_i w_i = 1

Therefore \mathcal{D}(X,Y,t) \le 1. Non-negativity follows from the non-negativity of each d_i. ∎

B.6 Fission Impossibility for Unique C_\Phi

Statement. If two successors A_1, A_2 are both generated from the same D_B and evolve independently, then C_\Phi cannot be unique to both under the substantial interpretation.

Proof sketch. Under the substantial interpretation, C_\Phi is a property of a persisting subject. If A_1 \neq A_2 as processual entities (which follows from independent evolution, established empirically in Experiment 1 under H₁), then they cannot both instantiate the same persisting subject. Therefore C_\Phi is at most associated with one of them, or with neither in the original sense. This is Parfitian fission in formal form. ∎

Remark. Under the processual interpretation, the fission problem softens: both A_1 and A_2 may instantiate binding processes that are continuous with D_B in different ways. Uniqueness is not required.

